          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "Guidance on starting a new blood pressure medicine describes a monthly review until the target is reached. Once blood pressure is stable, reviews move to every three to six months. This comes from the \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\" guidance, in its follow-up section.\n\nThat same section says that after starting a medicine that acts on the renin-angiotensin system, a clinician also checks kidney function and potassium. Those are the blood tests the guidance names.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not give a specific interval for every blood pressure medicine type. They also do not describe how a clinician would decide the right schedule for any one person. Please talk with the prescribing clinician about when your own check-ups should happen.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
